## Hash and lifecycle boundary

Evaluation hashes nothing and the generated crate embeds no SHA-256 digest.
The Rust types implement the standard `Hash` trait because the accepted Entity,
Value Object, and Enum realization protocols require equality-compatible hash
behavior. The evaluator never invokes that trait, and it is not lifecycle or
authority.

A SHA-256 source aggregate and artifact digests appear only in
`DOMAINSPEC-INSTANTIATION-RECEIPT.json` so the static generator can detect
changed source or generated bytes. Those digests are not Program identity, Fact
identity, authority, adoption, repository state, or a lifecycle clock.
`GovernanceProgram.id` is the designated Entity identity; `version` records its
explicit lifecycle revision.

The DomainSpec source pins baseline `{{BASELINE_ID}}` explicitly. A newer global
baseline does not refresh this kernel implicitly. Changing that pin is a
deliberate instantiation upgrade and requires revalidation of this bounded
target only.

## Claim ceiling

This proves a deterministic closed DomainSpec-to-Rust instantiation and local
behavior for one candidate. It is not a generic policy language, an accepted
Governance runtime, an enforcement binding, a Constitution, authority, release,
deployment, or permission to affect work.
